<chapter xml:id="ch-framing-models" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Framing and Models</title>

  <introduction>
    <warning>
      <p>
        WORK IN PROGRESS
      </p>
    </warning>

    <p><xref provisional="WORK-IN-PROGRESS"/></p>
  
    <p>
      This section lays out a plan for a potential research methods paper. 
      The cK&#162; vocabulary (Balacheff &amp; Gaudin, 2009) and the two-
      digit correctness coding (Gerami et&#160;al., 2024; Deng et&#160;al., 
      2026) are the framework we adopt; the contribution is the
      <em>operationalization</em> of that framework into an auditable,
      reproducible, human-in-the-loop pipeline, together with an
      empirical evaluation of whether the pipeline helps. Reproducible
      LLM-assisted qualitative coding is the part the field has not
      solved, and that is where the novelty lives. The formalism is
      <em>scaffolding</em>, not the headline.
    </p>

    <p><term>Potential Titles</term></p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <q>
            Auditable Human-in-the-Loop Coding of Student Mathematical Justifications:
            Operationalizing a cK&#162;-Anchored Scheme with an LLM-Assisted Pipeline.
          </q>
        </li>
        <li>
          <q>
            A Human-in-the-Loop Pipeline for Theory-Anchored Coding of
            Mathematical Justification in Short-Answer Reading Responses.
          </q>
        </li>

      </ol>
    </p>

    <p><term>Contribution</term></p>
    <p>
      The contribution is stated in three parts, and the paper must
      deliver all three.
      <dl>
        <li><title> Framing </title>
          <p>
            Adapt the cK&#162; vocabulary to <em>response-level traces</em>
            without claiming that a single response is a conception.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li><title> Tool Design Methods </title>
          <p>
            Formalize the coding workflow into auditable stages that
            preserve uncertainty, out-of-vocabulary cases, multiple
            matches, and reviewer overrides, with per-run provenance.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li><title> Empirical Methods </title>
          <p>
            Evaluate whether the pipeline improves coding consistency,
            speed, transparency, or disagreement diagnosis against ordinary
            spreadsheet coding. The paper needs results, not just
            architecture.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p><term>Central Claim</term></p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        We present and validate a human-in-the-loop pipeline for coding
        short-answer mathematical justifications.  The pipeline operationalizes
        a cK&#162;-anchored coding scheme by extracting response-level episodes
        of justification, matching them to a question-specific codebook, and
        preserving uncertainty, disagreement, and reviewer overrides as
        analyzable data.  The tool is not designed to infer complete student
        conceptions from individual responses; instead, it supports reliable,
        auditable analysis of the evidence for control structures that appears
        in student writing.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p><term>Research Questions</term></p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Reliability.</term> To what extent does the pipeline reproduce
            human screening and coding decisions on a held-out set of student
            short-answer responses?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Auditability.</term> What kinds of uncertainty, ambiguity, and
            codebook disagreement does the pipeline surface for human review?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Efficiency.</term> How much coding labor is reduced or
            reorganized when the pipeline identifies clear cases, ambiguous cases,
            OOV cases, and multiple-match cases?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Methodological insight.</term> What error types account for
            disagreement between the pipeline and human coders, and what do those
            errors reveal about the codebook, controlled vocabulary, and coding
            protocol?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      Checkpoint tracking for this plan lives in
      <c>dev/claude-build-tasks/proteus-sa-expansion-build-checklist.md</c>;
      the checklist section formerly included in this chapter was merged
      into that file and deleted.
    </p>

  </introduction>

	<section xml:id="subsec-paper-proposal-framework">
		<title>Analytic Framework</title>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			We adapt the cK&#162; vocabulary to model response-level traces rather than complete cognitive conceptions. By treating responses as episodes of justification consisting of a criterion, an inferential link, and a judgment, we establish a cognitively aligned framework for automated reasoning extraction. The framework is the <term>Episode Model</term> and the primary analytic object is a <term>coded episode</term>.  For a student response <m>x</m> to a question <m>q</m>, the pipeline extracts a sequence of local episodes:
			<md>
				E(x)=\langle e_1,e_2,\ldots,e_n\rangle.
			</md>
		</p>

		<p>
			Each episode has the form <md>e_k=(C_k, I_k, J_k)</md>, where <m>C_k</m> is the criterion the student cites, <m>I_k</m> is the inferential link that connects the criterion to the judgment, and <m>J_k</m> is the judgment the student draws. The three parts are written in that order &#8212; criterion, inferential link, judgment &#8212; everywhere in this book. An episode is a visible trace of justification, not a claim that a conception has been observed.
		</p>

		<p>
			The cK&#162; model supplies the theoretical vocabulary, but the response itself is not treated as a complete conception.  Instead, it enters at the question level as an analytic frame
			<md>
				K_q=(P_q,R_q,L_q,\Sigma_q),
			</md>
			where <m>P_q</m> is the relevant problem class, <m>R_q</m> is the set of relevant operators, <m>L_q</m> is the representation system, and <m>\Sigma_q</m> is the question-level controls. The student's response provides evidence for elements of this frame, but it does not determine the whole frame.
		</p>

		<p>
			Complete episodes receive a two-part correctness code:
			<md>
				\operatorname{code}(e_k)=(c^1_k,c^2_k).
			</md>
			The first digit <m>c^1_k</m> records whether the cited criterion is true of the adopted reference state. The second digit <m>c^2_k</m> records whether the inference from criterion to judgment is mathematically valid for the question. Incomplete episodes are not forced into this two-digit scheme; they are preserved as partial evidence or routed to human review.
		</p>

		<p>
			Two secondary structures are included to solve recurring coding problems.
			<dl>
				<li>
					<title>Nesting and independence</title>
					<p>
						If one episode's judgment becomes the criterion for another episode, the episodes are chained or nested. We write <m>e_i \sqsubseteq e_j</m> when episode <m>e_i</m> functions as a sub-step inside episode <m>e_j</m>, and <m>e_i\parallel e_j</m> when two episodes are independent for coding purposes.
					</p>
				</li>

				<li>
					<title>Coding policy for nested episodes</title>
					<p>
						A policy
						<m>\pi</m> determines whether nested episodes are coded as both parent and child, parent only, or child only. This is a coding convention, not a fact the mathematics determines by itself. The group must fix <m>\pi</m> before producing a validation dataset.
					</p>
				</li>
			</dl>
		</p>

		<p>
			The distinction between <term>justification quality</term> and <term>goal coverage</term> is retained, but it is secondary to the episode code.  A response may contain locally sound episodes while failing to answer the whole prompt.  Thus, an episode-level code of <m>(1,1)</m> certifies the local justification, not necessarily the completeness of the response.
		</p>

		<p>
			This section records the working model of how we code student responses against control structures &#8212; it is the one coding-model document for the project. We borrow vocabulary from the cK&#162; model of a conception (Balacheff &amp; Gaudin, 2009) because that vocabulary already names the parts we care about.  The aim throughout is a notation precise enough that a coder &#8212; human or automated &#8212; and an engineer can talk about the same object.
		</p>

		<p>
			Two sources are layered here, and it is worth keeping them distinct because they do different work.
			<dl>
				<li><title> The descriptive skeleton </title>
					<p>
						From Balacheff &amp; Gaudin (2009) we take the quadruplet <m>(P, R, L, \Sigma)</m> that models a <term>conception</term>. This describes the student's knowing as an equilibrium; it is a description, not a score, and not a process.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li><title> The correctness overlay </title>
					<p>
						From Gerami et&#160;al. (2024) we take the two-digit correctness code.  This is a <em>normative</em> annotation we place on top of the description; it asks whether the student's reasoning was right, a question the cK&#162; model itself does not ask.
					</p>
				</li>
			</dl>
			The episode layer itself &#8212; reading a single response as a sequence of coded episodes &#8212; is our own addition; it is not part of cK&#162;, and we treat it as an addition rather than a reading of the source.
		</p>

		<p>
			Within a response we distinguish two kinds of move.  An <term>operation</term> is generative: it builds or transforms an object and outputs a new object (a system, a matrix, a parametric solution).  A <term>control</term> is evaluative: it reads a feature of the current state and returns a judgment (consistent or not; unique or infinitely many).  An episode records an enacted control; an operation is not itself coded.  This split is convenient but it is a <em>stipulation we impose for coding</em>, not a distinction cK&#162; hands us cleanly: Balacheff allows an operator to take the form of a theorem applied in an inference, so an inferential operator and a control can look alike.  The cases where the line is genuinely blurred are exactly the sub-step disputes the nesting policy <m>\pi</m> exists to resolve.
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<subsection>
		<title> The conception layer </title>

		<p>
			Following Balacheff &amp; Gaudin, a conception is modeled by the quadruplet <m>(P, R, L, \Sigma)</m>, where
			<dl>
				<li>
					<title> <m>P</m>, problems </title>
					<p> the class of situations the conception is able to resolve. </p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<title> <m>R</m>, operators </title>
					<p>
						the means that perform actions or transform symbolic, graphical, and linguistic representations.  Note that the letter <m>R</m> denotes <em>operators</em>, not representations; this is Balacheff's assignment and we keep it.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<title> <m>L</m>, representation system </title>
					<p> the symbolic and linguistic means that carry the work and its feedback. </p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<title> <m>\Sigma</m>, control structure </title>
					<p>
						the means by which choices are made, decisions taken, and judgments expressed &#8212; the validation dimension.
					</p>
				</li>
			</dl>
		</p>

		<p>
			It is important that this layer describes the <em>student's</em> conception, and that a conception is an equilibrium rather than a trajectory.  The episode layer adds the notion of a sequence of local justification moves read from a single response; that notion is useful for us but is not itself a cK&#162; construct, and we treat it as an addition rather than a reading of the source.
		</p>
	</subsection>

	<subsection>
		<title> The correctness overlay </title>

		<p>
			Correctness is an annotation laid on episodes, kept deliberately
			outside the conception layer.  It is defined against a
			<term>normative reference</term>: the true state of the
			mathematics, and the per-question set <m>\mathcal{T}</m> of
			inferential links that are valid (which criteria genuinely entail
			which judgments for this question).
		</p>

		<definition>
			<idx> correctness code </idx>
			<p>
	For an episode <m>e_k = (C_k, I_k, J_k)</m> that is
	<term>complete</term> (its inferential link and judgment are both
	stated), the <term>correctness code</term> is the pair
	<md>
		\operatorname{code}(e_k) = (c^1_k, c^2_k) \in \{0,1\}^2,
	</md>
	where <m>c^1_k</m> records whether the criterion <m>C_k</m> is
	correct against the true state, and <m>c^2_k</m> records whether
	the inferential link <m>I_k</m> belongs to <m>\mathcal{T}</m>.  An
	episode whose link or judgment is missing is <term>incomplete</term>;
	it is flagged <q>link not satisfied</q> and is not scored.
			</p>
		</definition>

		<p>
			Two consequences are worth stating, because they keep the overlay
			honest about what it certifies.  First, the two digits are
			independent: the criterion and the judgment of an episode can both
			be sound while the inference between them is wrong, so
			<m>c^1_k = 1</m> together with <m>c^2_k = 0</m> is an ordinary and
			important case, and the link is the thing <m>c^2</m> grades.
			Second, scoring every stated episode <m>(1,1)</m> certifies the
			<term>quality of the justification offered</term>, which is not
			the same as the <term>correctness of the solution</term>.  A
			response can leave the question's goal uncovered while every
			episode it does state is sound, and a criterion can be read
			correctly off work that was itself faulty, so the proposition it
			records is false against the true state.  We therefore keep two
			axes apart: justification quality, which the code measures episode
			by episode, and solution correctness, which additionally asks
			whether the sound episodes <em>cover</em> the question's goal on a
			correctly computed state.
		</p>
	</subsection>

	<subsection>
		<title> A worked example </title>

		<p>
			To make the vocabulary concrete, we trace one real response
			through the pipeline's stages &#8212; screen, extract, match,
			dispose, with human review as the standing layer above them.
			Checkpoint <m>1.4.6\mathrm{A}</m> presents an augmented matrix in
			reduced row echelon form, states that the corresponding system is
			consistent, and asks the student to decide whether the solution is
			unique and to identify the basic and free variables.  The
			question's goal is therefore a classification of the solution
			set.  Consider the response
			<q>we only have 2 pivot points, so one variable will be free</q>,
			and read it stage by stage.
			<dl>
	<li> <title> Screen </title>
	<p>
		The response cites a feature of the matrix and draws a conclusion
		from it &#8212; a justification worth coding &#8212; so screening
		admits it: <c>INCLUDE</c>.
	</p> </li>
	<li> <title> Extract </title>
	<p>
		Reading off and counting the pivots is an operation: it transforms
		the given matrix into a count &#8212; two pivots among three
		variables &#8212; and, being generative rather than evaluative, it
		is not itself coded.  On top of that count sits one episode
		<md>
			e_1 = (C_1, I_1, J_1),
		</md>
		with criterion <m>C_1</m> &#8220;two pivots, three
		variables,&#8221; inferential link <m>I_1</m> &#8220;fewer pivots
		than variables, so a variable is unconstrained,&#8221; and judgment
		<m>J_1</m> &#8220;one variable is free.&#8221;  The student stops
		here; note for later that <m>J_1</m> is not yet the classification
		the task asks for.
	</p> </li>
	<li> <title> Match </title>
	<p>
		Matching classifies the episode against the codebook: counting
		pivots against variables to force a free variable is
		<m>\mathrm{CS6}</m>.  Locating the pivots was an operation, not a
		control, so it is not coded.  A coder might be tempted to add a
		second episode <m>e_2</m> of type <m>\mathrm{CS7}</m> for
		&#8220;a free variable exists&#8221; &#8212; but that proposition
		<em>is</em> <m>J_1</m>, the judgment already produced by
		<m>e_1</m>: its content is consumed inside <m>e_1</m> rather than
		standing on its own.  This is the nested case
		<md>
			e_2 \sqsubseteq e_1,
		</md>
		detected here rather than resolved.
	</p> </li>
	<li> <title> Dispose </title>
	<p>
		Disposition hands the nested pair to the policy <m>\pi</m>: under
		a &#8220;parent only&#8221; policy we keep <m>e_1</m> and drop the
		contained <m>e_2</m>, which is where the open question is actually
		decided.  The surviving episode is complete, so it is eligible for
		the two-digit annotation &#8212; and that annotation is the human
		review layer's work: checking the criterion against the true state
		and the inferential link against <m>\mathcal{T}</m>, the reviewer
		records
		<md>
			\operatorname{code}(e_1) = (1, 1).
		</md>
	</p> </li>
			</dl>
		</p>

		<p>
			The example also shows why the two axes are kept apart.  Although
			<m>e_1</m> scores <m>(1,1)</m>, the response never reaches the
			question's goal: it establishes that a free variable exists but
			never draws the classification the task asks for.  The
			justification offered is sound; the solution is incomplete because
			the stated episodes do not <em>cover</em> the goal.
		</p>

		<p>
			For contrast, take the shorter response
			<q>We cannot determine the value of free variable</q>
			to the same checkpoint.  Extraction yields a criterion
			&#8212; a free variable is present &#8212; but the continuation
			restates what &#8220;free&#8221; means rather than inferring a
			solution-set type, so the inferential link and the judgment are
			absent.  Matching tentatively reads this as <m>\mathrm{CS7}</m>,
			but the episode is incomplete: it has a <m>C</m> with no
			<m>I</m> or <m>J</m>.  Disposition therefore does not assign a
			code; the episode is flagged <q>link not satisfied</q> and routed
			to a human.  This is the incomplete-episode branch of the
			pipeline, and it is deliberately kept outside the
			<m>(c^1, c^2)</m> annotation.
		</p>
	</subsection>

	<subsection>
		<title> Correspondence with the stored schema </title>

		<p>
			The pipeline stores episodes in the frozen v3 schema, where a
			response's extracted reasoning lives in the <c>chains</c> and
			<c>chain_steps</c> tables and each question's valid inferences
			live in <c>question_controls</c>.  The table below fixes the
			correspondence between the document's terms, the symbols above,
			and the stored columns, so that a coder and an engineer reading
			the same episode can point at the same fields.
		</p>

		<table xml:id="table-episode-schema-correspondence">
			<title>Episode Model terms, symbols, and schema locations</title>
			<tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
				<row header="yes">
					<cell>Document term</cell>
					<cell>Symbol</cell>
					<cell>Schema location</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>criterion</cell>
					<cell><m>C_k</m></cell>
					<cell>the chain-step <c>premise</c> slot; <c>question_controls.criterion</c></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>inferential link</cell>
					<cell><m>I_k</m></cell>
					<cell><c>question_controls.normative_link</c></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>judgment</cell>
					<cell><m>J_k</m></cell>
					<cell>the chain-step <c>conclusion</c>; <c>question_controls.conclusion</c></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>valid-inference set</cell>
					<cell><m>\mathcal{T}</m></cell>
					<cell>the per-question rows of <c>question_controls</c></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
		</table>

		<p>
			One naming collision deserves a note, because it cannot be fixed
			where it occurs.  The schema's column comment glosses
			<c>normative_link</c> with the letter <q>L</q>, which collides
			with <m>L</m> = representation system in the cK&#162; quadruplet
			<m>(P, R, L, \Sigma)</m> &#8212; indeed the neighboring
			<c>reads_representation_id</c> comment uses <q>L</q> in the
			representation sense.  In the Episode Model the inferential link
			is <m>I</m>, never <m>L</m>.  The SQL baseline is frozen, so the
			fix of record is this table plus a note in the durable reference
			record (<c>dev/claude-build-tasks/important-references.md</c>);
			<c>shortanswer.sql</c> is not edited &#8212; even its comments
			&#8212; without explicit owner approval.
		</p>
	</subsection>

	<subsection>
		<title> Notation summary </title>

		<p>
			<dl>
	<li> <title> <m>(P, R, L, \Sigma)</m> </title>
	<p> a conception: problems, operators, representation system, control structure. </p> </li>
	<li> <title> <m>K_q = (P_q, R_q, L_q, \Sigma_q)</m> </title>
	<p> the question-level analytic frame. </p> </li>
	<li> <title> <m>E(x) = \langle e_1, \ldots, e_n \rangle</m> </title>
	<p> the episode sequence extracted from response <m>x</m>. </p> </li>
	<li> <title> <m>e_k = (C_k, I_k, J_k)</m> </title>
	<p> a coded episode: criterion, inferential link, judgment. </p> </li>
	<li> <title> <m>e_i \sqsubseteq e_j</m>, <m>\ e_a \parallel e_b</m> </title>
	<p> sub-step containment (nesting); independence. </p> </li>
	<li> <title> <m>\pi</m> </title>
	<p> the coding policy resolving nested episodes (a convention the group fixes, not a fact the mathematics decides). </p> </li>
	<li> <title> <m>\mathcal{T}</m> </title>
	<p> the per-question set of valid inferential links. </p> </li>
	<li> <title> <m>\operatorname{code}(e_k) = (c^1_k, c^2_k)</m> </title>
	<p> the two-digit correctness annotation: criterion against the true state; link against <m>\mathcal{T}</m>. </p> </li>
			</dl>
		</p>
	</subsection>
</section>
<section>
  <title>Tool Design</title>

  <p>
    The workflow is a sequence of small, auditable stages, each
    reading a simple table and writing a slightly richer one:
    <term>screening</term>, <term>extraction</term>,
    <term>matching</term>, <term>disposition</term>, and
    <term>human review</term>. Three properties make this more than an
    implementation note and are worth foregrounding as the
    methodological result.
    <dl>
      <li><title> Per-run provenance </title>
        <p>
          Every row the pipeline writes references a <c>run_id</c> tagged
          with prompt version, model identifier, vocabulary version, and
          codebook version. Re-running with a new prompt produces a
          parallel set of rows rather than an overwrite, so two
          configurations can be compared on the same corpus. This is what
          makes the LLM-assisted coding <em>reproducible</em>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li><title> Question-relative correctness </title>
        <p>
          Valid criterion-to-judgment links are stored per question (in
          <c>question_controls</c>), so correctness is judged against the
          task, not against a global codebook assumption.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li><title> Preserved uncertainty and human authority </title>
        <p>
          Ambiguity, multiple plausible matches, out-of-vocabulary terms,
          and incomplete (<q>link not satisfied</q>) episodes are
          preserved and routed, not forced into a code. AI-proposed
          entries are gated by an acceptance flag that only a human can
          flip, and every override is logged.
        </p>
      </li>
    </dl>
  </p>
  
  <p>
    The methodological contribution the paper claims from this design is
    the operationalization of qualitative coding as an auditable
    computational sequence of stages &#8212; screening, extraction,
    matching, disposition, and human review &#8212; that preserves
    uncertainty instead of hiding it: ambiguous cases, out-of-vocabulary
    terminology, multiple matches, and reviewer overrides are treated as
    data.  Complex phenomena like nested reasoning are resolved via the
    explicit coding policy <m>\pi</m>, a convention the group fixes, not
    a structure the software imposes.
  </p>
</section>
<section xml:id="sec-paper-proposal-study-design">
  <title>Proposed Study Design</title>

  <p>
    The evaluation is the part that converts a tool description into a
    research result. A credible design has the following components.
    <dl>
<li> <title> A research corpus </title>
<p>
  Real short-answer reading responses drawn from several
  questions, de-identified, with IRB status recorded. The current
  repository fixtures demonstrate ingestion but are test data, not
  research evidence, and must not be reported as such.
</p> </li>
<li> <title> A human ground truth with a reliability ceiling </title>
<p>
  Two independent coders plus adjudication. Report percent
  agreement and <m>\kappa</m> (or Krippendorff's <m>\alpha</m>)
  for include/exclude decisions and for control-structure
  assignment. Human&#8211;human agreement is the ceiling against
  which the tool is measured.
</p> </li>
<li> <title> A three-system comparison </title>
<p>
  Human-only coding, a deterministic pattern baseline, and the
  LLM-assisted pipeline. Report precision, recall, and
  <m>F_1</m> for include/exclude, and code-level agreement for
  control-structure assignment, each against the adjudicated gold
  set.
</p> </li>
<li> <title> An ablation </title>
<p>
  Literal extraction versus interpretive extraction, the
  experiment the repository documentation already identifies as a
  natural first comparison.
</p> </li>
<li> <title> An error taxonomy </title>
<p>
  Screening errors, extraction errors, matching errors,
  missing-vocabulary / OOV errors, nested-control ambiguity, and
  human-codebook instability, each with counts and representative
  cases.
</p> </li>
<li> <title> A productivity and auditability result </title>
<p>
  Time saved, number auto-accepted, number escalated, override
  rate, and how often the tool surfaces a needed codebook
  revision.
</p> </li>
    </dl>
  </p>

  <p>
    Two design controls are not optional. First, to avoid
    <term>anchoring bias</term>, reviewers should make a first-pass
    judgment with candidate codes hidden, and this protocol must be
    reported. Second, the ethics and consent basis for using real
    student data must be stated explicitly.
  </p>

  <p>
    The empirical contribution the paper claims from this design is the
    evaluation itself: whether the pipeline improves coding reliability
    and consistency, efficiency, transparency, or disagreement diagnosis
    relative to the current human-only workflow, measured against human
    coders on the adjudicated gold set.
  </p>


  <p>
    The empirical study compares three coding workflows:
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Human-only coding.</term> Two independent human coders apply
          the codebook using the current spreadsheet or manual workflow.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Deterministic baseline.</term> The rule-based screener,
          connector-based extractor, pattern matcher, and rule-based
          disposer are run without an LLM.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>LLM-assisted pipeline.</term> The same pipeline architecture
          is used, but selected language-heavy stages are replaced by
          LLM-assisted components that produce structured, reviewable output.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <p>
    The dataset includes real short-answer reading-question responses
    from several questions. The sample is stratified by question and by expected
    coding difficulty. At minimum, it should include:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          clean single-control responses;
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          blank or no-effort responses;
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          bare-answer responses with no justification;
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          responses with multiple possible control structures;
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          responses with nested or chained reasoning;
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          responses using terminology outside the current vocabulary; and
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          responses where the final answer is correct but the inferential
          link offered for it is incomplete or invalid.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    The validation report 1:
    <dl>
      <li>
        <title>Human agreement</title>
        <p>
          Percent agreement and Cohen's <m>\kappa</m> or Krippendorff's
          <m>\alpha</m> between independent human coders, before
          adjudication.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>Pipeline agreement</title>
        <p>
          Accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 for screening decisions, and
          code-level agreement between the pipeline's candidate codes and the
          adjudicated human codes.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>Disposition distribution</title>
        <p>
          The number and percentage of responses assigned to
          <c>auto_accept</c>, <c>standard_review</c>,
          <c>terminology_flag</c>, <c>no_match</c>, and
          <c>escalate</c>.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>Override analysis</title>
        <p>
          The rate at which human reviewers confirm, add, remove, replace,
          or reject the pipeline's candidate codes.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>Error taxonomy</title>
        <p>
          A qualitative analysis of screening errors, extraction errors,
          codebook-matching errors, OOV errors, nested-control ambiguity,
          question-coverage failures, and human-codebook instability.
        </p>
      </li>
    </dl>
  </p>

  <p>
    The validation report 2:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Construct a representative corpus of short-answer reading
          responses spanning multiple question types, ensuring the valid
          reasoning for a control is question-relative rather than universal.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Deploy a three-way comparison study: human-only coding, a
          deterministic/traditional NLP baseline, and the LLM-assisted
          pipeline.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Employ two independent human coders with an adjudication phase,
          reporting precision, recall, F1 scores for screening decisions,
          and inter-rater reliability metrics (Cohen's <m>\kappa</m> or
          Krippendorff's <m>\alpha</m>) for control-structure assignments.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Publish a comprehensive error taxonomy categorizing failures into
          screening errors, extraction errors, codebook-matching errors,
          out-of-vocabulary (OOV) instances, and nested-control ambiguities.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</section>
<section xml:id="sec-publication-proposal-cl"
	 xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Final Thoughts</title>

  <subsection>
    <title> Threats, scope, and a horizon </title>

    <p>
      Three honest limits should be stated in the paper rather than
      discovered by a reviewer. The study is a <em>case study</em> on one
      question family and a small number of courses; generality is not
      claimed. The LLM is treated as unreliable at exactly the
      mathematical judgment correctness coding depends on, which is why
      human authority and the deterministic baseline are load-bearing
      rather than decorative. And the coder's discipline of not
      supplying a judgment the student did not make is preserved
      throughout, so incomplete responses are reported as partial
      evidence, never completed by the tool.
    </p>

    <p>
      A horizon, not a claim for this paper: the same
      criterion-and-link decomposition that localizes a coding decision
      also localizes <em>where</em> a student's reasoning breaks, which
      is the natural ingredient for instructor-facing draft feedback on
      reading questions that are presently graded by hand. That
      extension keeps a human in the loop and is the sensible next study
      once the coding pipeline is validated; it is named here only to
      mark the direction.
    </p>

    <p>
      The project is promising, but several risks should be addressed directly
      in the paper.
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Risk 1: Overclaiming what the tool detects</title>
          <p>
            Avoid saying that the tool detects student conceptions.  Say instead
            that it extracts and organizes evidence-bearing traces of
            justification that may support later interpretation across tasks.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Risk 2: Treating LLM output as a final code</title>
          <p>
            The pipeline should produce candidate codes and review dispositions,
            not final research claims.  Human review remains part of the method.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Risk 3: Letting formalism outrun evidence</title>
          <p>
            This risk was resolved by removal: on 2026-07-17 the owner cut
            the reasoning-graph formalism from the framework entirely, and
            the Episode Model is the single canonical framework.  The paper
            foregrounds episodes and the explicit coding policy; no graph
            machinery remains to outrun the evidence.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Risk 4: Validating against an unstable codebook</title>
          <p>
            The group must freeze the codebook, vocabulary, and nested-episode
            policy before measuring tool performance.  Otherwise, disagreement
            may reflect moving definitions rather than tool failure.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Risk 5: Reporting only aggregate performance</title>
          <p>
            Accuracy alone is not enough. The paper should include qualitative
            examples of errors and disagreements because those examples are where
            the methodological contribution becomes visible.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-paper-proposal-bottom-line">
    <title>Bottom Line</title>

    <p>
      The group has developed and validated a reproducible workflow for
      theory-anchored, human-in-the-loop coding of mathematical justification.
      The episode model supplies the analytic unit, the pipeline supplies the
      auditable workflow, and the empirical study supplies the evidence that the
      method improves or clarifies the research process.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>

</chapter>